# What produced flutter/web/sqflite_sw.js and flutter/web/sqlite3.wasm.
# Written by hand from the output of, run in flutter/:
#
#   dart run sqflite_common_ffi_web:setup
#
# ONE command writes BOTH files, so they are recorded together — regenerating
# the worker silently replaces the wasm as well, and the wasm is the larger
# change (the setup downloads it from a sqlite3.dart release the package
# version chooses, see sqlite3-release below).
#
# Why this file exists: the worker carries no version marker of its own
# (`grep 1.1.2 sqflite_sw.js` finds nothing), pubspec.yaml pins a caret range,
# and the client encoding that talks to this worker is compiled into
# main.dart.js. So `flutter pub upgrade` could move the client while the
# committed worker stayed frozen, and nothing would notice. CI checks the
# version below against flutter/pubspec.lock — the resolved truth, not the
# range — and the hashes against the committed bytes:
#
#   ./scripts/check-sqflite-provenance.sh
#
# It ships. That is deliberate rather than an oversight: a SharedWorker is
# matched by (origin, name, URL) and the URL is the constant `sqflite_sw.js`,
# so a tab left open across a deploy keeps the OLD worker alive. Per-file
# content hashing cannot close that (issue #256), but `curl
# https://botvinnik.app/sqflite_sw.version` at least answers which package the
# thing serving that tab came from.

package         sqflite_common_ffi_web
version         1.1.2
package-sha256  32dc484518fec883cf0d1aa85c767f7cde6757729480b3d8d20e0fac247ef161
sqflite_sw.js   sha256:a97c2da7e87f22bc0fd5eae0def1dc8dee4314014acc6e88fbdfc1ebb066777a
sqlite3.wasm    sha256:8766db025f5d5b6f24c6a51cc9dec843ab7a7720e3dfa5b47d70d33db96d506b

# Not checked, recorded because they explain the bytes.
#
# `dart`: the setup COMPILES the worker locally with dart2js rather than
# downloading a prebuilt one, so — as with the Go version in
# vendor/retro/BUILD.txt — a different SDK can produce different bytes from
# the same package. Unlike retro, CI does NOT rebuild and compare: the runner
# takes Flutter's floating `stable` channel, so a derived check would go red on
# an SDK release rather than on a real drift. Verified once by hand instead —
# regenerating at 1.1.2 on the SDK below reproduced the committed bytes exactly.
#
# `sqlite3-release`: the GitHub release the setup fetched sqlite3.wasm from.
# Chosen by the package, printed by the setup, and recorded here because
# nothing else in the repo says which SQLite is inside that wasm.

dart            3.12.2
sqlite3-release sqlite3-3.1.2
